<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=nuclear-fission">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nuclear fission</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1938">1938</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nuclear fission is a reaction in which the nucleus of an atom splits into two or more smaller nuclei, releasing a very large amount of energy; it was discovered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> in December 1938, when the chemists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-hahn">Otto Hahn</a> and Fritz Strassmann found barium among the products of uranium bombarded with neutrons. Lise Meitner and Otto Robert Frisch explained the result in January 1939, and Frisch named the process after the division of living cells. The extra neutrons it releases make a chain reaction possible, the basis of nuclear power and nuclear weapons.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nuclear-fission.jpg" alt="Nuclear fission" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: J Brew · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nuclear_Fission_Experimental_Apparatus_1938_-_Deutsches_Museum_-_Munich.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>In fission the nucleus of an atom splits into two or more smaller nuclei, usually with neutrons and gamma rays, and gives off a very large amount of energy. The energy released by the fission of one uranium-235 atom is about 100 million times that of burning one carbon atom in air. Most fissions are binary, with two fragments of unequal size, typically in a mass ratio of about 3 to 2. Three fragments appear in two to four events per thousand. Because the fragments are different elements from the parent atom, fission is a form of nuclear transmutation. Spontaneous fission, with no neutron to start it, was discovered in 1940 by Georgy Flyorov, Konstantin Petrzhak and Igor Kurchatov.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>The discovery was made in 1938 in the buildings of the Kaiser Wilhelm Society for Chemistry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. The chemists <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-hahn">Otto Hahn</a> and Fritz Strassmann proved on 19 December 1938 that a fission reaction had taken place. The physicist Lise Meitner and her nephew Otto Robert Frisch explained it in theory in January 1939, and Frisch named it by analogy with the division of living cells. In February 1939 Hahn and Strassmann predicted that further neutrons were set free, which opened the way to a chain reaction.</p><p>The Hungarian physicist Leó Szilárd had first thought of a chain reaction driven by neutrons in 1933. On learning of Hahn's result from Eugene Wigner in January 1939 he urged Fermi and Frédéric Joliot-Curie not to publish, lest the Nazi government take notice. Fermi agreed to hold back, but Joliot-Curie's team reported in April 1939 in the journal Nature that uranium gave off 3.5 neutrons per fission.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Uranium-233, uranium-235 and plutonium-239 can sustain a chain reaction. In a controlled form it runs power reactors, research reactors and breeder reactors, which make more fissile fuel than they use. An uncontrolled form is the basis of the atomic bomb. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> began an all-out effort to make such weapons in late 1942, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Manhattan Project</a>, led by General Leslie R. Groves, with J. Robert Oppenheimer directing its science, worked at Hanford, Oak Ridge and Los Alamos. The first device was fired in the Trinity test in July 1945, and in August 1945 a uranium bomb and a plutonium bomb were used on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hiroshima">Hiroshima</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>.</p><p>Fission also occurred in nature. At Oklo in Gabon sixteen sites show self-sustaining chain reactions about 2 billion years ago, found by Francis Perrin in 1972 and suggested by Paul Kuroda in 1956.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nuclear fission” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1938</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Berlin</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Otto Hahn · Fritz Strassmann · Lise Meitner · Otto Robert Frisch</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Hahn and Strassmann found the evidence in Berlin; Meitner and Frisch, who explained it in January 1939, were then in exile.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/radioactivity">Radioactivity<span>1896</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/quantum-mechanics">Quantum theory<span>1900</span></a></div><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Led to</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/chicago-pile-1">Nuclear reactor<span>1942</span></a><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/manhattan-project">Atomic bomb<span>1945</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=nuclear-fission">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
